{
  "scenes": [
    {
      "scene": "intro",
      "narration": "Today we're looking at fzf, the command-line fuzzy finder, one of the most starred CLI tools on GitHub. Let's start from absolutely nothing and clone it.",
      "start": 2.29,
      "end": 14.95
    },
    {
      "scene": "install",
      "narration": "Installation is a single script. We'll use the bin-only mode so it doesn't touch our shell config, and notice it just downloads a prebuilt binary, no compilation needed.",
      "start": 14.95,
      "end": 30.92
    },
    {
      "scene": "filter",
      "narration": "The core of fzf is fuzzy matching. In filter mode you can see exactly what the matcher does: given a list of fruits, the query \"an\" fuzzy matches banana, mango and orange, any line containing those letters in order.",
      "start": 30.92,
      "end": 46.79
    },
    {
      "scene": "outro",
      "narration": "And that's the thirty-second tour. fzf earns its stars: one-script install, instant startup, and it composes with everything on your system.",
      "start": 46.79,
      "end": 51.8
    }
  ],
